      en: "The Gwayi River (also Gwaai) is approximately 300 km long in northwestern Zimbabwe. It rises near Bulawayo and flows northwest, joining the Zambezi River. The river traverses semi-arid savannas and experiences strong seasonal fluctuations. Its catchment area covers about 15,000 km². It supports agriculture, particularly cotton and maize irrigation. Part of the Zambezi basin, it hosts hippos and crocodiles. Riverine vegetation includes acacia and mopane woodlands. Historically, the river served as a trade route for indigenous people.",
